    long: "Differential privacy is a property of an algorithm, not of a dataset. The algorithm adds calibrated noise so that the presence of any one unit of data makes little difference to what comes out, and epsilon measures how little: the smaller it is, the stronger the protection and the noisier the answers. That tension between privacy and usefulness is the whole practical problem, and no tooling removes it. NIST's SP 800-226, final in March 2025, gives a structured way to question a claim: a differential privacy pyramid and a set of privacy hazards, the common pitfalls that appear when the mathematics meets an implementation. The curriculum tests a claim on three things: the unit of privacy (event, user or user-day), the total epsilon budget across all queries and what happens when it runs out, and the implementation. It is the right instrument when an organisation publishes or shares aggregate statistics repeatedly from data it controls, against an adversary with strong outside information.",
    why: "An epsilon on a slide reassures without promising anything. Asking for the unit, the total budget and its owner turns a privacy claim into one that can be checked.",
    related: ["composition", "epsilon-budget", "sensitivity", "k-anonymity", "membership-inference"],
    mode: "motus",
    sources: [{ label: "NIST SP 800-226, Guidelines for Evaluating Differential Privacy Guarantees (March 2025)", url: "https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-226.pdf", checked: READ3 }],
