    term: "Call Graph",
    domain: "Application Security",
    short: "A map of which functions in a program can call which others, used to decide whether vulnerable code can be reached from an entrypoint.",
    long: "Reachability analysis walks a call graph from a program's entrypoints to a vulnerable function: if no path exists, the vulnerable code is present but never runs. The curriculum's triage drill starts there, asking whether the vulnerable function is reachable in the organisation's call graphs before recommending anything. Its KEV, EPSS and reachability Step states the limit: reflection, dynamic dispatch, deserialization, configuration-driven class loading, native calls and plugin architectures all break static call-graph analysis, generally in the unsafe direction, by hiding paths that exist. Trusting a call-graph result over those mechanisms without saying so is one of the errors the Step names. Smart contract analysers such as Slither build call graphs for the same purpose.",
