    term: "Vulnerability Class",
    domain: "Application Security",
    short: "A family of flaws that share a cause, such as broken object level authorisation or memory unsafety. Changing the condition behind it fixes every instance at once.",
    long: "The curriculum reads every finding at three levels. Instance: this endpoint leaks another tenant's invoice. Class: broken object level authorisation. Originating condition: the framework lets a developer fetch by primary key without ever passing the caller's identity into the query. Fixing the instance fixes one endpoint; changing the condition, so the data access layer requires an actor argument that cannot be defaulted, makes every endpoint written afterwards safe by construction. Memory unsafety is the one major vulnerability class with a proven industrial cure. Not every class can be deleted. Business logic flaws, such as a price that can be driven negative or a refund flow that can be replayed, have no framework condition to change, because the condition is the domain itself. The rule for buying tools follows: name the vulnerability class a tool addresses and the class it leaves uncovered, because teams buy tools for the classes tools can find and are breached through the classes tools never could.",
