    short: "The boundary within which one root of trust issues and verifies identities. In SPIFFE it is the first part of every workload's ID.",
    long: "SPIFFE describes the trust domain as the trust root of a system: all workloads identified in the same trust domain receive identity documents that can be verified against its root keys. A SPIFFE ID is a URI of the form spiffe://trust-domain/path, and the curriculum's guidance is typically one trust domain per security environment; SPIFFE's documentation advises keeping different locations, and environments with different security practices such as staging and production, in distinct trust domains. The idea reaches beyond SPIFFE. Mutual redundancy inside a single trust domain is not redundancy against an adversary who owns the domain, which is why the guides put security logs, and the keys that wrap other keys, in a separate one. A useful test for any control is whether it sits in the same trust domain as the thing it protects; if it does, one compromise takes both.",
